   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "title: \"Parallelizing your code\"\n",
    "description: \"Strategies to speed up your code\"\n",
    "categories: [Python, multiprocesses, C, numpy, bytes, threads, cores]\n",
    "date: 09-15-2026\n",
    "image: python_numpy_c_thumb.png\n",
    "draft: false\n",
    "tbl-cap-location: top\n",
    "number-sections: true\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "Once upon a time, computers had only a single core, with a single physical thread, and therefore, they could only run a single operation sequentially.\n",
    "\n",
    "Nowadays, computers have multiple cores (physical or not), and each can have as many threads as the manufacturer wants, but usually, it goes up to 8, while 2 is very common in intel and AMD processors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, it is useful to take advantage of the power that machines have, and running a single program in parallel.\n",
    "\n",
    "> \"Parallel programming can be defined as a model aims to create compatible programs with ready environments to execute code simultaneously.\" Jan Palach - Parallel Programming with Python\n",
    "\n",
    "In order to run a program in parallel, we can think of different ways of doing it:\n",
    "\n",
    "-   Multithreads\n",
    "-   Multiprocessing\n",
    "-   Concurrence\n",
    "-   Async I/O\n",
    "\n",
    "In order to use one of these methods, we need to firstly understand the problem we need to solve to find the right tool.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Multithreads\n",
    "\n",
    "A multithread program is a program that can use multiple threads to run the tasks that needs to be solved. So, the thread itself runs your program. A characteristics of the multiple threads, it is that they all share the same memory and same process and are managed by the OS. A downside of using multithreads it is that only a single thread can execute the code, while all others needs to wait the lock to get relased. It occurs because Python has GIL (Global Interpreter Lock), that allows only a single thread to be executed inside a process at time. In most recent versions, there is a possibility to release this restrain, but you can face more race conditions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, in the usual scenario, multiple threads run concurrently: only one can execute, while all others need to wait.\n",
    "\n",
    "The advantage of using a multithreaded code, it is that if we are facing an Input/Output (I/O) operation (an operation, for example, that needs to wait the server to respond), the threads can operate in the interleave mode.For example: + Thread 1 makes a request in the server and waits the response (The OS places a lock on this thread and picks the second one). + Thread 2 makes the request in the server and waits the response (This thread receives lock). + Thread 1 collects the data from server and finishes. + Thread 2 collects the data from server and finishes.\n",
    "\n",
    "Even though the threads are not running at the same time, that is the impression for the user because the changes between the threads may occurs in milliseconds.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{markdown, code-fold=\"false\"}\n",
    "#| eval: false\n",
    "#| code-fold: false\n",
    "\n",
    "A queue objet in Python, can have two methods:\n",
    "+  `get` will retrieve an element from the queue. Once it is `got`, it is no longer in the `queue`.\n",
    "+  `queue` will show all elements in a queue.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "https://github.com/dunossauro/live-de-python/blob/main/codigo/Live052/decomposicao.py\n",
    "\n",
    "Python does not process real \"parallel\" threads, they are threads concurrent. So, they search for a specific time window that they can solve the task while the others are waiting.\n",
    "\n",
    "Threads basically are workers generated within your program that perform tasks inside a single core. If you have more than one thread, you have multithreading. In order to arrange all of them, you need to set up an `event`, so every object in a queue respects this event (because every thread needs to stay organized in the same core); also a `queue`, so it will be submitted to the `thread`. Having a `queue` and the `event` setup, you can generate your pool of `threads`, choosing as many as you want, and they will complete the task for you; finally, you have to send the `kill` \"method\", so the thread dies at the end and frees memory.\n",
    "\n",
    "They all share the same memory.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is concurrent processing.\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## Multiprocessing\n",
    "\n",
    "When we run a program in multiprocess, it means that we start the program from a parent process (`os.getppid()`), and from it, multiple processes starts. This multiple child processes runs independently (not concurrently), in parallel (at the same time) because they are GIL free (because are the different process), they do not share the same memory.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is usefull when you have a CPU operation and you can take usage of as many cores you have on your machine. Even though you can start a multiprocessing code in a single core, the usefulness of this technique is achieved when you allocate each process in a different CPU core.\n",
    "\n",
    "For simple and light weight operations (I/O), it may take longer than multithreading and it uses more memory, because the objects needs to copied on each process.\n",
    "\n",
    "A good experiment is to watch how many process are running on you OS.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Multithreads and Multiprocessing in OS\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, take a look on `htop` screenshot:\n",
    "\n",
    "![](htop.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "We have 198 tasks running, which in this case means processes, and they are using 1526 threads in total, but only 1 is running (all the others 1525 are sleeping, waiting to be called).\n",
    "\n",
    "So, a OS can start as many processes as it wants (independently of the number of cores), and for each process, having multiple threads associated to id - making it a multithreaded multiprocess operation.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{markdown, code-fold=\"false\"}\n",
    "#| eval: false\n",
    "#| code-fold: false\n",
    "\n",
    "Each process is a program\\script\\\"thing\" that has isolated memory and share the processing queue running independently.\n",
    "\n",
    "`os.getpid()` returns the current process\n",
    "`os.getppid()` returns the parent process, the process that originated the current process.\n",
    "\n",
    "Concurrent are executions in a program inside the same process. Parallel are different processes.?\n",
    "\n",
    "You can use the library `multiprocessing` to start a new process from a `function`. Besides, it can share elements from a `Queue`, using the `Queue` of the parent process. The `Queue` is a process.?.\n",
    "\n",
    "In here, each `worker` is a `process`.\n",
    "\n",
    "You can start as many processes as you want on your single core, or distribuite it in many cores.\n",
    "\n",
    "The function that will be executed is `dumped` using `pickle` into each process, and it needs to be serializable (for example, it could not be a closure).\n",
    "\n",
    "It runs without GIL (Global Interpreter Lock) restrictions: only 1 thread can execute the same piece of code at the same time: two threads can not save two objects using the same function, even if the objects are different at the same time. Or it can be understood as well a lock (block) of an assynchronous programming.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is parallelized processing.\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## Collections\n",
    "\n",
    "-   Python has different data `structures`: dict, list, string, tuples, sets. Each one with their own limitation in order to gain speed.\n",
    "-   But It also has `collections`, which are containers of data that allow users to extend the original data structures to their own use, such as `UserDict`, `Userlist` and `UserString`. When heritage from `collections.abc` your new objects/class will work like a builtin in Python. Collections are an example of monkey patch.\n",
    "-   `Monkey Patch` is a technique in programming to change the default behavior of a piece of code (function, element, class) at runtime.\n",
    "-\n",
    "\n",
    "<!-- This is used for `mutable` Python objects -->\n",
    "\n",
    "## Concurrency with futures\n",
    "\n",
    "Inicia em 10:30\n",
    "\n",
    "Future -\\> something that it is going to be processed in the future. It may start now, or in the future. Differently from lazy evalution that evaluates only when needed, a Future will evaluate parallely or concurrently to the code in sometime between today and when the code is needed (or when the code waits for this result). So, it can start a thread or a process.\n",
    "\n",
    "Dentro do concurrent.futures tem uma classe chamada Executor, com três métodos principais: - Submit, que retorna Future, que é uma promise. que é uma promessa de que algo vai ser feito no futuro, ou está sendo feito agora. Mas a ideia principal é que não vai dar um lock no processo atual. - Map, vai pegar uma função e vai mandar para um iterável qualquer, Podendo definir timeout e chunksize. - Shutdown, com o wait = True diz que vai rodar a future até terminar.\n",
    "\n",
    "-   Executor objects\n",
    "-   ThreadPools:\n",
    "    -   Tarefas bloqueantes de I/O vão dar bypass no GIL - E aqui o asyncio é matador.\n",
    "    -   Threads atuam concorrentemente dentro de um processo. Não são paralelas.\n",
    "-   ProcessPools\n",
    "-   Future objects\n",
    "\n",
    "Promise -\\> é a promessa que um processamento vai ser feito, e será calculado.\n",
    "\n",
    "`with` sempre garante que a seção de código dentro do with vai ser executado.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Concurrency with asyncio\n",
    "\n",
    "Concorrência: 2 coisas não são feitas ao mesmo tempo; Paralelismo: 2 coisas são feitas ao mesmo tempo;\n",
    "\n",
    "Código síncrono: Faz a execução inteira, esperando ela acabar, até iniciar a outra. Código assíncrono: Posso começar a execução, parar, e voltar do ponto de onde eu parei, para aí então terminar.\n",
    "\n",
    "Executa tarefas assíncronas em loop de forma gerenciada.\n",
    "\n",
    "ROTINA: São os trechos de código que vc vai verificar se vai fazer tarefa A ou tarefa B, voltando isso em LOOPL\n",
    "\n",
    "Um código python retorna com gerador é quando ele response com yield, e não return. E o yield é um lazy evaluation.\n",
    "\n",
    "yield from e await são uma forma de consumir uma corrotina. A corrotina tem que ser função assíncrona.\n",
    "\n",
    "aiohttp::ClientSession -\\> manipular os requests. Roda os requests de forma assíncrono, sem bloqueio. O request é bloqueante. Roda de forma síncrona.\n",
    "\n",
    "async - define que a função é assíncrona await - define para esperar o comando terminal.\n",
    "\n",
    "## What is the difference between multithreads and async io?\n",
    "\n",
    "## Old text\n",
    "\n",
    "In computer we have tasks. The tasks are done by the worker. The worker can be a thread, a process, for example. What a worker is depend on the context of your code.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Books\n",
    "\n",
    "-   Python Fluente\n",
    "-   Python Cookbook\n",
    "\n",
    "what is the pratical difference between map and map_async: from multiprocessing import Pool def soma_2(x) result x+2 workers = Pool(5) result = workers.map(soma_2, range(100)) result.wait() result.get()\n",
    "\n",
    "result2 = workers.map_async(soma_2, range(100)) result2.wait() result2.get()"
   ]
